# What Literate does not promise

A tool is easier to trust when its limits are stated beside its strengths. Literate
keeps explanation and implementation together, plans deterministic outputs, maps
generated code back to fragments, and supports several publication forms. None of
those capabilities removes the underlying tradeoffs of indirection, generated
artifacts, external tools, or host filesystems.

This chapter is not a list of apologies. It draws the boundary around the system's
actual contract so authors can decide when the method fits and contributors can
recognize proposals that require new guarantees.

## It does not remove indirection

Named fragments let explanatory order differ from source-file order. The cost is
that a statement's surrounding generated code may live elsewhere in the book. A
reader sometimes has to follow a reference, inspect a family with several
contributions, or view the assembled source.

Good names, parent outlines, definition links, indexes, and the Fragment Explorer
reduce that cost. They do not make it disappear. Excessive fragmentation can still
turn a chapter into a trail of links, and a family amended in many distant places
can be harder to understand than one coherent definition.

Literate also does not infer the best fragments automatically. Syntax boundaries
are not necessarily conceptual boundaries. Choosing names and deciding where an
idea belongs remain authorial work.

## Generated files remain real artifacts

The book is authoritative for the program fragments it contains, but generated
source still matters. Compilers, language servers, package builders, debuggers, and
reviewers consume it. Literate keeps generated files in the repository to support
bootstrapping and ordinary tools.

This creates obligations rather than a second source of truth:

- generated files can become stale and must be checked;
- direct edits to them will be overwritten and must be moved back to the book;
- generated diffs need review because they reveal assembly consequences;
- removed or renamed roots can leave orphaned files that are not deleted
  automatically;
- merge conflicts may appear in both authoritative and derived views.

A successful generation updates changed destinations; it does not perform general
repository garbage collection or decide that an unplanned file is safe to delete.

## Text generation is not language understanding

Fragment expansion is intentionally language-neutral. References are recognized by
Literate's fragment syntax even when they appear inside what the generated language
would call a string or comment. Escaping an opening delimiter is therefore part of
the literate source contract.

Literate does not parse every generated language to prove that the assembled source
is syntactically or semantically correct. That is the job of its compiler, tests,
and language tools. Templates and wrappers can also introduce generated text that
has no direct editable source span.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Literate[Literate structure] --> Tangle[Assembled text]
    Tangle --> Compiler[Language compiler or interpreter]
    Compiler --> Behavior[Program behavior]
    Literate --> Validate[Fragment graph and output policy]
    Validate --> Tangle
```

The upper path shows separate responsibilities. Literate validates its own graph
and output rules; the target-language tool validates the assembled program. One
cannot substitute for the other.

## Projection cannot make every generated position editable

Language projection lets a server analyze assembled source while the author edits
fragments. The mapping is exact for literal source segments and deliberately
conservative elsewhere. Wrapper text, indentation inserted during expansion,
reference seams, and positions spanning several mappings may have no safe reverse
translation.

Repeated fragments create a deeper ambiguity. One source interval can appear at
several generated positions. Read-only services can select an occurrence in stable
order and return useful hover or navigation information. A generated edit is held
to a stronger rule: it must map completely through the exact requested occurrence
and remain confined to current source. If applying it would amplify one server edit
through repeated expansion, Literate rejects it.

The result is intentionally incomplete language intelligence. Some valid server
answers are omitted, and some refactorings must be performed in the literate source
rather than accepted as projected edits. The system prefers no edit to a plausible
edit at the wrong place.

Nor does direct protocol support imply compatibility with every language server.
Servers differ in initialization, project discovery, dynamic capabilities,
filesystem expectations, and extensions. Tested configurations establish evidence
for those configurations, not a universal LSP guarantee.

## Safety boundaries are not sandboxes

Workspace Trust, path validation, bounded messages, private temporary directories,
disabled shell escape, and process termination all reduce risk. They do not make
trusted project code or external compilers safe to run on hostile input.

Self-host verification executes a freshly built engine. PDF publication invokes
programs capable of reading files and executing substantial logic. A browser or
language server is a large external process. These features are intended for
trusted projects and explicitly selected tools.

Likewise, a temporary directory is an isolation aid, not a security boundary by
itself. A child process can access resources permitted to the user unless the
operating system supplies stronger confinement outside Literate.

## Output transactions are not filesystem-wide atomicity

Literate plans all outputs before writing and uses a shared transaction protocol to
preflight, stage, install, roll back, and report recovery material. This protects
against many ordinary exceptions. It does not provide database semantics.

In particular, the transaction has no crash-durable journal, no lock against
external writers, and no instant at which every reader on every filesystem must see
all new files simultaneously. A power loss can interrupt a batch. Another process
can race a preflight check. Filesystem providers may expose weaker link or metadata
information than a local POSIX filesystem.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Plan[Complete semantic plan] --> Preflight[Inspect destinations]
    Preflight --> Stage[Stage changed files]
    Stage --> Install[Install and back up]
    Install --> Cleanup[Remove private recovery files]
    External[Power loss or competing writer] -. outside full control .-> Preflight
    External -. outside full control .-> Install
```

The dotted arrows mark interference Literate can detect or recover from in some
cases but cannot abolish. Callers must serialize writers, avoid concurrent manual
changes, and inspect reported recovery paths rather than assuming rollback always
completed.

## External dependencies remain external

Core parsing and planning do not require a graphical editor, but richer features
rely on other programs and resources. Publication may require a document converter,
a TeX toolchain, suitable fonts, and an explicitly selected browser for diagrams.
Language intelligence requires configured language servers. Packaging relies on the
repository's build toolchain.

Literate validates and invokes these dependencies; it does not silently download or
install them. It cannot guarantee that a font contains every glyph, that a compiler
accepts every project, or that a future external release preserves behavior. Locked
project dependencies improve repeatability, while system tools still need explicit
preflight and supported-environment testing.

Portable publication also has a bounded meaning. Explicit font and license
companions can travel with an export, and local supported images can be copied, but
Literate does not determine whether a license permits redistribution. It does not
turn remote images, arbitrary raw graphics, or every browser resource into a
self-contained book.

## Not every feature works in every workspace

The shared engine and workspace filesystem adapter can serve more environments than
process-owning integrations. Remote providers can participate in project capture,
generation, and preview through the editor's filesystem API, subject to the
provider's semantics.

Features that launch local processes have a narrower boundary. Direct language
servers require a trusted local file workspace. Editor-driven publication requires
a trusted local desktop POSIX environment. Remote, web, and Windows extension hosts
are rejected for these process-owning features; displaying a `.literate` document
does not grant the missing authority or process model.

This difference is intentional. Pretending that a remote URI is a local path would
be less portable, not more. Unsupported environments should retain read-only or
core capabilities where safe and explain why a process-owning feature is absent.

## Screen and print are related, not identical

A shared publication model gives HTML, editor preview, LaTeX, and PDF the same
contributions, annotations, and logical targets. Each medium still has different
layout and navigation. A link on screen may become a page reference in print;
interactive fragment expansion has no direct paper equivalent; line and page
breaks depend on typography.

The print bridge supports a controlled document vocabulary rather than arbitrary
Markdown, HTML, TeX, or browser behavior. Unsupported or ambiguous constructs are
reported instead of being copied into executable TeX. Images and diagrams have
explicit format and resource limits. Font companions instead receive path,
manifest, presence, and file-kind validation; they do not currently have dedicated
byte or count budgets.

Successful PDF compilation proves that the selected tools produced an accepted
file. It does not prove perfect typography. Missing glyphs, overfull lines,
unfortunate page breaks, figure scale, and the readability of dense code still
need human review. Screen success also does not prove print success, or vice versa.

## Determinism is not permanence

The same accepted snapshot and policy produce the same semantic result, but an edit
can intentionally change identity and order. Renaming a fragment changes its target.
Moving a contribution can change encounter order. Inserting a duplicate heading or
contribution can change later disambiguating ordinals.

Literate promises deterministic identities under a fixed accepted input. It does
not promise that all links survive arbitrary structural edits without review.
Explicit author identities would be needed wherever edit-stability must outweigh
derived identity.

Deterministic planning also does not guarantee byte-identical output from every
external renderer. Tool releases, installed fonts, and platform libraries can
affect a final PDF or image even when the renderer-neutral plan is unchanged.

## Bounds do not guarantee every project will finish

Explicit budgets protect protocol messages, caches, browser exploration,
publication requests, assets, subprocess time, logs, and compiler products. They
fail conservatively at those boundaries. They are not a proof that total CPU,
memory, disk, or filesystem latency is bounded for every accepted project.

Large source sets and expansions still require proportional work. External
processes have internal resources that Literate cannot fully account for. An
unresponsive filesystem can delay operations outside a child-process deadline.
Resource policy makes failure safer and more predictable; it does not create
unlimited capacity.

## The honest promise

The limits above leave a substantial and useful contract:

- one captured project has one declared document and contribution order;
- fragment errors and unsafe output plans do not become partial generation success;
- the engine returns plans rather than performing hidden writes;
- hosts compare and commit complete plans through explicit policies;
- generated-language mappings prefer exact omission over ambiguous invention;
- screen and print publication share semantic identities while respecting their
  media;
- self-host verification checks a real generation fixed point without claiming to
  prove the whole application;
- unsupported environments and exceeded limits are reported rather than disguised.

That contract is narrower than “prose and code can never drift” or “all tools work
everywhere.” It is also stronger because it can be tested. The final chapter draws
lessons from building and maintaining a system around this boundary, and asks what
continued literate development should optimize for.
